<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arsuf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arsuf is the site of an ancient and medieval town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast north of modern Herzliya, Israel, known in antiquity as Apollonia. Taken by the crusaders in 1101, it was the seat of a lordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and in 1191 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured and destroyed it in 1265, and its ruins form Apollonia National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arsuf.jpg" alt="Arsuf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: אור פ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arsur_Fortress1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Crusade (1189–1192) revealed the limits of Ayyubid power against determined European opponents: Saladin lost Acre to the combined forces of Richard I of England and Philip II of France in 1191 and was defeated in the field at <b>Arsuf</b>, though he retained Jerusalem under the Treaty of Jaffa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Fatimids and extending the kingdom along the coast with the help of Genoese, Pisan and Venetian fleets, taking <b>Arsuf</b> and Caesarea in 1101, Acre in 1104 and Beirut and Sidon in 1110, and pushing south-east of the Dead Sea, where he built the castle of Montréal in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He went on to win at <b>Arsuf</b> but never retook Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching down the coast, Richard's army withstood harassing attacks before a crusader charge broke Saladin's forces at <b>Arsuf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arsuf</b>, the ancient Apollonia, surrendered to Baldwin I in 1101 with the help of a Genoese fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-arsuf">Lordship of Arsuf</a></li><li><blockquote>The family survived the catastrophe and in the 13th century dominated Beirut, Jaffa, <b>Arsuf</b> and the Kingdom of Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Lordship of Ibelin</a></li></ul></section>
